<p>At the destination, the handover of a two‑wheeler or motorcycle is a structured process that protects both the carrier and the customer. The first task is a daylight condition re‑inspection that references the original pickup photos. By comparing the current exterior and mechanical condition against the images taken at dispatch, any new dents, scratches, or mechanical issues are immediately identified. The inspector records each discrepancy with a photo, noting the time of day to avoid shadows or glare that could obscure defects. This step ensures that the vehicle’s condition is documented in real time and that the customer can verify that the bike arrives as it left the warehouse.</p>

<ul style="list-style: none; padding-left: 0; margin: 18px 0;">
<li style="margin-bottom: 10px; display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 5px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;"></i><span>Step 1 – Visual & Photographic Check: The inspector walks the motorcycle, taking close‑up photos of the front, side, and rear. These are compared side‑by‑side with the pickup images stored in the dispatch system.</span></li>
<li style="margin-bottom: 10px; display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 5px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;"></i><span>Step 2 – Odometer Verification: The current mileage is read and cross‑checked against the odometer reading recorded on the LR bilty. Any variance triggers an immediate audit; the driver must provide a written statement and the customer signs a mileage discrepancy form if applicable.</span></li>
<li style="margin-bottom: 10px; display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 5px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;"></i><span>Step 3 – Functional Test: The engine is started, the brakes are applied, and the horn is tested to confirm mechanical integrity. A short test ride on a safe, closed circuit is performed if time allows.</span></li>
<li style="margin-bottom: 10px; display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 5px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;"></i><span>Step 4 – Condition Sign‑Off Sheet: A standardized sign‑off sheet lists all inspected items, the inspector’s name, the customer’s signature, and the date/time stamp. The sheet is scanned into the digital delivery system and emailed to the customer for their records.</span></li>
<li style="margin-bottom: 10px; display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 5px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;"></i><span>Step 5 – Final Handover: The customer receives the keys, original documents, and a copy of the signed sign‑off. Any outstanding issues are logged in the carrier’s incident report, and the carrier’s supervisor is notified for follow‑up.</span></li>
</ul>

<p>By following this rigorous daylight re‑inspection, odometer verification, and signed condition sign‑off protocol, the carrier maintains transparency, reduces post‑delivery disputes, and upholds the quality standards expected in the two‑wheeler logistics market.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>



